--TEST--
Core\Str::reverse reverses grapheme clusters, so a subject strrev would come back from as invalid UTF-8 comes back here as the same characters in the opposite order
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
One divergence, and it is the same **unit** disagreement `slice` and `replaceRange` have with `substr` — read here on a member that has no offsets at all, so the unit is the *only* thing the two can differ about. `Core\Str::reverse` walks grapheme clusters (`nvs_stdlib::granularity::DEFAULT`, `rule:types/string-is-utf8`) and `strrev` walks bytes. **Against `strrev`.** `"aé中👍"` is `61c3a9e4b8adf09f918d`, and `strrev` answers `8d919ff0adb8e4a9c361` — every multi-byte sequence written backwards, starting on a continuation byte. That is not well-formed UTF-8, and so is not a value Novis can hold at all: every Novis `string` is valid UTF-8 and `rule:types/conversion` makes `bytes as string` the checked conversion precisely so a half-decoded fragment cannot arrive by accident. `Core\Str::reverse` answers `f09f918de4b8adc3a961`, the same four clusters in the opposite order with each one intact. So this is not a case of two defensible answers: the twin's answer for any subject outside ASCII is mojibake, and a PHP program that reached for `strrev` over UTF-8 text was already producing it. There is nothing here for a port to want back, which is why no compatibility spelling is offered. **Against the code-point unit.** PHP has no `mb_strrev` to compare against — the idiom is `implode('', array_reverse(mb_str_split($s)))` — and it would still part from this member, one step later than `strrev` does: `"a👍🏽b"` is 4 code points and 3 clusters, so a code-point reversal emits U+1F3FD before U+1F44D and renders a bare skin-tone modifier followed by an unmodified thumbs-up, where `Core\Str::reverse` moves `👍🏽` as one character. `"cafe\u{0301}"` splits the same way: the combining acute leads the result and attaches to nothing, where the member keeps `e`+U+0301 together as the reversal's first cluster. `mb_str_split` is not callable on this leg in any case — the `php` on the Windows `PATH` has no `mbstring` — which is the second reason the expectation below is frozen rather than computed. **What does *not* part them is the involution**: reversing twice restores the subject on both sides, because reversing invalid bytes twice restores valid ones. That property is asserted against PHP over an ASCII table in the companion case, `str-reverse-matches-strrev`; what it cannot see, and what this file exists for, is which unit was reversed on the way.
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Spec § 1's `reverse` row over subjects where the byte, the code point and
// the grapheme cluster are three different counts. The agreeing half — the
// answers, the involution, the palindromes, the width and the hundred
// ordered pairs of the anti-homomorphism, all against `strrev` over an ASCII
// table — is in `str-reverse-matches-strrev`.

final class Show {
    // A case cannot echo a `bytes` (`rule:types/conversion`), and the byte order this
    // file is about is invisible in rendered text, so hex is how the unit
    // `strrev` counts in becomes readable without ever holding one of its
    // answers.
    public static function bytes(string $text): string {
        return Core\Encoding::toHex($text as bytes);
    }
}

// Three subjects, one per way the units part. `$mixed` has one code point per
// cluster and four different byte widths, so it separates the byte unit from
// the other two; `$tone` has a two-code-point cluster and `$combining` a base
// plus a mark, which is where the code-point unit stops agreeing as well.
string $mixed = "aé中👍";
string $tone = "a👍🏽b";
string $combining = "cafe\u{0301}";

// Each subject's cluster count beside the bytes it occupies. `strlen` reads
// the length of the hex string halved — ten, ten and six — and
// `Core\Str::length` reads four, three and four, which is already the whole
// disagreement stated in numbers before a single character has moved.
echo Core\Str::length($mixed), " ", Show::bytes($mixed), "\n";
echo Core\Str::length($tone), " ", Show::bytes($tone), "\n";
echo Core\Str::length($combining), " ", Show::bytes($combining), "\n";

// The reversals, rendered and in hex. Read each hex answer against the
// subject's hex two lines up: it is the same byte groups in the opposite
// order, never the same bytes in the opposite order, which is exactly the
// claim. `strrev`'s answer for these three is the second thing — every hex
// digit pair reversed — and is not a `string` in this language.
echo "[", Core\Str::reverse($mixed), "=", Show::bytes(Core\Str::reverse($mixed)), "]\n";
echo "[", Core\Str::reverse($tone), "=", Show::bytes(Core\Str::reverse($tone)), "]\n";
// `$combining` is rendered as hex alone, not beside itself: a decomposed
// `e`+U+0301 and a precomposed U+00E9 are indistinguishable in a text editor
// and are different strings, so an `--EXPECT--` block holding the rendered
// form would pin whichever one this file happened to be saved in.
echo "[", Show::bytes(Core\Str::reverse($combining)), "]\n";

// The two counts that say *reversal of clusters* rather than *some
// rearrangement that looks right*, over all twelve cells of three subjects
// by four offsets.
//
// * **Mirrored.** The cluster at offset `$index` of the reversal is the
//   cluster `$index + 1` back from the end of the subject — the reversal
//   read forwards is the subject read backwards, cell by cell. A byte
//   reverser fails every cell of every subject here; a code-point reverser
//   fails `$tone`'s and `$combining`'s.
// * **Whole.** Every one of those slices is one unit wide, so nothing was
//   split on the way through.
//
// Eleven of twelve, both times, and the twelfth cell is the same one in
// both: `$tone` has three clusters, so offset 3 is past its end. The
// reversal's fourth slice is empty — `slice`'s answer past the end rather
// than a throw — while `slice($tone, -4, 1)` reaches back further than the
// subject is long and clamps to the start, answering `a`.
array<string> $subjects = ["aé中👍", "a👍🏽b", "cafe\u{0301}"];
array<int> $indices = [0, 1, 2, 3];
int $mirrored = 0;
int $whole = 0;
int $cells = 0;
foreach ($subjects as string $subject) {
    foreach ($indices as int $index) {
        $cells = $cells + 1;
        if (Core\Str::slice(Core\Str::reverse($subject), $index, 1) == Core\Str::slice($subject, -$index - 1, 1)) {
            $mirrored = $mirrored + 1;
        }
        if (Core\Str::length(Core\Str::slice(Core\Str::reverse($subject), $index, 1)) == 1) {
            $whole = $whole + 1;
        }
    }
}
echo $mirrored, " ", $whole, " of ", $cells, "\n";

// The two properties `strrev` also holds, restated in this file's unit so
// the divergence above is not mistaken for a defect. Reversing twice is the
// identity, and a reversal answers as many clusters as it was given —
// three of three on both counts. `strrev` holds the first over these
// subjects too, and the second only when "as many" is read in bytes.
int $back = 0;
int $wide = 0;
int $seen = 0;
foreach ($subjects as string $one) {
    $seen = $seen + 1;
    if (Core\Str::reverse(Core\Str::reverse($one)) == $one) {
        $back = $back + 1;
    }
    if (Core\Str::length(Core\Str::reverse($one)) == Core\Str::length($one)) {
        $wide = $wide + 1;
    }
}
echo $back, " ", $wide, " of ", $seen, "\n";
--EXPECT--
4 61c3a9e4b8adf09f918d
3 61f09f918df09f8fbd62
4 63616665cc81
[👍中éa=f09f918de4b8adc3a961]
[b👍🏽a=62f09f918df09f8fbd61]
[65cc81666163]
11 11 of 12
3 3 of 3
